The current code you provided for evaluating a quadratic equation in PHP is already using the standard formula. However, we can optimize it by reducing the number of arithmetic operations and conditional statements. One way to do this is by using the following code:
```
$a = 2;
$b = 5;
$c = -3;
$discriminant = $b * $b - 4 * $a * $c;
if ($discriminant >= 0) {
    $sqrt = sqrt($discriminant);
    $root1 = (-$b + $sqrt) / (2 * $a);
    $root2 = (-$b - $sqrt) / (2 * $a);
    if ($discriminant == 0) {
        echo "Root: " . $root1 . "<br>";
    } else {
        echo "Root 1: " . $root1 . "<br>";
        echo "Root 2: " . $root2 . "<br>";
    }
} else {
    $realPart = -$b / (2 * $a);
    $imaginaryPart = sqrt(-$discriminant) / (2 * $a);
    echo "Root 1: " . $realPart . " + " . $imaginaryPart . "i" . "<br>";
    echo "Root 2: " . $realPart . " - " . $imaginaryPart . "i" . "<br>";
}
```
This code first checks if the discriminant is non-negative before computing the square root to avoid unnecessary arithmetic operations. It also eliminates the redundant `elseif` statement by combining the two cases where the discriminant is zero or non-zero.
To generate a table with a wider range of coefficients for a, b, and c, you can use the following code:
```
echo "<table>";
echo "<tr><th>a</th><th>b</th><th>c</th><th>Root 1</th><th>Root 2</th></tr>";
for ($a = -10; $a <= 10; $a++) {
    for ($b = -10; $b <= 10; $b++) {
        for ($c = -10; $c <= 10; $c++) {
            $discriminant = $b * $b - 4 * $a * $c;
            if ($discriminant >= 0) {
                $sqrt = sqrt($discriminant);
                $root1 = (-$b + $sqrt) / (2 * $a);
                $root2 = (-$b - $sqrt) / (2 * $a);
                if ($discriminant == 0) {
                    echo "<tr><td>" . $a . "</td><td>" . $b . "</td><td>" . $c . "</td><td>" . $root1 . "</td><td></td></tr>";
                } else {
                    echo "<tr><td>" . $a . "</td><td>" . $b . "</td><td>" . $c . "</td><td>" . $root1 . "</td><td>" . $root2 . "</td></tr>";
                }
            } else {
                $realPart = -$b / (2 * $a);
                $imaginaryPart = sqrt(-$discriminant) / (2 * $a);
                echo "<tr><td>" . $a . "</td><td>" . $b . "</td><td>" . $c . "</td><td>" . $realPart . " + " . $imaginaryPart . "i" . "</td><td>" . $realPart . " - " . $imaginaryPart . "i" . "</td></tr>";
            }
        }
    }
}
echo "</table>";
```
This code generates a table with rows for each combination of coefficients for a, b, and c. It uses nested `for` loops to iterate over the ranges of coefficients and evaluates the quadratic equation for each combination. The resulting roots are displayed in the table along with the coefficients. If the discriminant is negative, the code displays the roots in their complex form.

