Certainly! Here's an example of C# code that uses the System.IO namespace to find and replace a specific word in a text file, where the file has multiple lines and the word must be replaced only in specific columns. It also ensures that the replaced word is in bold font and the replaced text is highlighted in yellow color:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
namespace FindAndReplace
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Specify the file path
            string filePath = @"C:\example.txt";
            // Specify the word to find and replace
            string wordToFind = "oldWord";
            string wordToReplace = "newWord";
            // Specify the column range where the word should be replaced
            int startColumn = 10;
            int endColumn = 20;
            // Read the file into memory
            string fileContents = File.ReadAllText(filePath);
            // Split the file contents into lines
            string[] lines = fileContents.Split('\n');
            // Loop through each line
            for (int i = 0; i < lines.Length; i++)
            {
                // Check if the line has the specified column range
                if (lines[i].Length >= endColumn)
                {
                    // Extract the text in the specified column range
                    string textInColumnRange = lines[i].Substring(startColumn, endColumn - startColumn);
                    // Check if the word to find exists in the column range
                    if (textInColumnRange.Contains(wordToFind))
                    {
                        // Replace the word with the new word
                        string replacedText = Regex.Replace(textInColumnRange, wordToFind, $"<b><span style='background-color: yellow;'>{wordToReplace}</span></b>");
                        // Replace the text in the original line with the replaced text
                        lines[i] = lines[i].Substring(0, startColumn) + replacedText + lines[i].Substring(endColumn);
                        // Print the updated line
                        Console.WriteLine(lines[i]);
                    }
                }
            }
            // Write the updated file contents back to the file
            File.WriteAllText(filePath, string.Join("\n", lines));
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first specify the file path, the word to find and replace, and the column range where the word should be replaced. We then read the file contents into memory and split it into lines. We loop through each line and check if it has the specified column range. If it does, we extract the text in that range and check if the word to find exists in it. If it does, we replace the word with the new word and format it to be in bold font and highlighted in yellow color. We then replace the text in the original line with the replaced text and print the updated line. Finally, we write the updated file contents back to the file.

